# 12 - the core is handed a duplicate of the tunnel, and the descriptor is checked not the number
#
# The ioctl failure was a number, not a path: a closed ParcelFileDescriptor reports -1, the
# library turned a non-positive number into an unset `xray.tun.fd`, and proxy/tun then fell back
# to "0" — stdin — whose TUNGETIFF is `inappropriate ioctl for device`. A number can also be
# reused by whichever file the process opened next, which is why `fd >= 3` alone is not enough
# to call a tunnel open. So the plugin now asks the descriptor itself
# (`descriptor.fileDescriptor.valid()`) in both places it decides to use one — the start, and the
# cached tunnel it would otherwise reuse on a tap — and hands the core a descriptor this start
# owns: `descriptor.dup()`, closed in a `finally` once the native call returns, because
# proxy/tun duplicates what it is given (F_DUPFD_CLOEXEC) and needs the caller's copy no longer.
# The library stops clearing the variable for a bad number and returns an error string instead:
# a caller with no tunnel to give must not start a core that will ioctl stdin, and the message
# says which number was wrong.

